All right, welcome back to 006.

2
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:18,420
Today we start a totally new section of the class.

3
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:23,600
Up till now, we've mostly been showing you really cool and powerful algorithms,

4
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:28,200
sorting algorithms, graph algorithms, data structures, trees,

5
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:31,960
lots of good stuff that you can apply to solve tons of algorithmic problems,

6
00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:36,879
either by reducing to the things, data structures that we showed you,

7
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,180
or reducing to graph problems that we showed you,

8
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:41,540
or by modifying those algorithms a bit.

9
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:45,200
Today we're going to start a new section on algorithmic design,

10
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:50,300
how to from scratch come up with a polynomial time algorithm to solve a problem.

11
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:54,840
So this is a, and in particular we're going to talk about

12
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:57,840
an algorithmic design paradigm called dynamic programming,

13
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,120
which is extremely powerful.

14
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:02,800
It's probably the most powerful algorithmic design paradigm,

15
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:04,940
very general, can solve lots of problems.

16
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:10,440
It's a particular type of recursive algorithm design.

17
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:15,040
And in general, this class, all of algorithms,

18
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:18,000
is about recursive algorithm design at some level

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,700
because we want to write constant size pieces of code

20
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:23,860
that solve problems of arbitrary size.

21
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:25,120
We have some problem size n,

22
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:28,160
and we're trying to write 100 lines of code or whatever

23
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:30,520
some constant amount that doesn't depend on the problem size.

24
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:34,560
We have one algorithm that solves all instances of the problem.

25
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:39,340
And so we have to write code that is recursive or uses loops

26
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:42,000
or somehow reuses the instructions that we give the computer.

27
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:46,580
And you may know you can convert any algorithm based on loops

28
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:48,440
into an algorithm using recursion.

29
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:51,940
And we're going to take the recursive view today in particular

30
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:55,640
because it fits very well with our proof by induction technique,

31
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:57,140
which we've used throughout this class.

32
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:01,280
But also because it gives us some structure

33
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:03,479
on how different subproblems relate

34
00:02:03,479 --> 00:02:05,840
in something called a subproblem graph

35
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:06,940
that we'll be talking about today.

36
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:10,660
And so we're going to start out with, in general,

37
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:12,820
how do we design recursive algorithms?

38
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:16,120
That's sort of the overall encompassing everything.

39
00:02:16,359 --> 00:02:19,840
We have thought very hard to come up with a cool acronym

40
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,920
for this paradigm, which we invented, called SortBot.

41
00:02:23,519 --> 00:02:24,120
Thanks, Jason.

42
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:26,480
And so we'll talk about...

43
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:27,700
It's not actually for sorting.

44
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:28,140
It's just...

45
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:33,299
It's just an acronym for subproblems, relations,

46
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:38,359
topological order, base case, original problem, and time.

47
00:02:39,019 --> 00:02:44,140
But it's an acronym that will help you remember all the steps you need

48
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:45,720
in order to specify a recursive algorithm.

49
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:49,400
And dynamic programming is going to build on this template

50
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:53,060
by adding one new idea called memoization,

51
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,960
which is just the idea of reusing work that you've done before.

52
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:58,140
And that's going to let us solve the problem.

53
00:02:58,160 --> 00:02:59,240
And that's going to let us solve tons of problems.

54
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:01,140
And...

55
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:02,380
Let's see.

56
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:03,160
I don't...

57
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:04,820
Let's get into it.

58
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:08,160
So we'll start out today with SortBot.

59
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:13,000
So here is SortBot down the column here.

60
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:15,579
This is a recursive algorithm design paradigm.

61
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:19,079
And in general, what we're going to do is take the problem

62
00:03:19,079 --> 00:03:20,359
that we actually want to solve

63
00:03:20,359 --> 00:03:24,079
and split it up into lots of possible subproblems.

64
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:26,079
And so the first part is to define

65
00:03:26,079 --> 00:03:27,160
what the heck are the subproblems.

66
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:27,780
Subproblems.

67
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:30,660
And in general, we'll want some polynomial number of them.

68
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:34,220
But it's pretty open-ended what these look like.

69
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:38,620
And the hardest part, usually, in defining a recursive algorithm

70
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:40,660
is figuring out what the subproblems should be.

71
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:42,980
Usually, they're related to the problem you want to solve.

72
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:45,180
Often, the problem you want to solve,

73
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:47,180
this is actually near the last step,

74
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:48,760
the original problem you're trying to solve

75
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:51,519
is often one of these subproblems.

76
00:03:51,519 --> 00:03:53,260
And then you use the smaller subproblems

77
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:56,660
in order to build up the final original problem.

78
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:57,700
But sometimes, at the end, you need to figure out, oh, well,

79
00:03:57,700 --> 00:03:58,540
I'm going to do this.

80
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:00,040
And then you need to take a bunch of subproblems

81
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:02,079
and combine it into your original problem.

82
00:04:02,079 --> 00:04:04,420
You can think, one analogy you can think of here

83
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:06,700
is divide and conquer algorithms, which

84
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:09,100
also had this kind of style.

85
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:12,280
But more generally, we're going to relate different subproblem

86
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:16,260
solutions with some recursive structure,

87
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:19,339
some recurrence relation.

88
00:04:19,339 --> 00:04:21,579
This is just a recursive algorithm

89
00:04:21,579 --> 00:04:24,740
that defines how to solve one problem in terms

90
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:27,700
of smaller subproblems for some notion of subproblems.

91
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,940
And so you can take these subproblems,

92
00:04:29,940 --> 00:04:31,180
and you can sort them out.

93
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:33,580
And you can also divide them into subproblems.

94
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:35,380
And you can look at the topological order

95
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:36,460
of the subproblems.

96
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:38,320
And you can see that the topological order

97
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:40,099
is a little bit smaller.

98
00:04:40,099 --> 00:04:43,060
And this is given by the topological order.

99
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:45,760
So if we think of the subproblems as a graph,

100
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:49,620
and we draw an edge between.

101
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:52,159
So the vertices of the graph are subproblems.

102
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:54,539
The edges are the dependencies between those subproblems.

103
00:04:54,539 --> 00:04:57,219
Then what we'd like is the topological ordering,

104
00:04:57,220 --> 00:05:00,420
and the topological order is the topological order.

105
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:02,320
And these are the dependencies between them

106
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:04,960
in this recursive relation forms a DAG.

107
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:06,440
We want it to be acyclic.

108
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:09,640
Otherwise, you have an infinite loop in your recursive calls.

109
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:13,440
If you have a cycle, you'll never terminate.

110
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:16,600
And so to make sure that these dependencies

111
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:19,480
between subproblems given by this recurrence relation

112
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,900
is acyclic, one way to do that is

113
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:24,420
to specify a topological order.

114
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:26,020
Or you could prove it some other way.

115
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:26,540
But often it's just a for loop.

116
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:27,120
Just do it in this order.

117
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:27,220
OK.

118
00:05:27,220 --> 00:05:30,980
Then, of course, any recursive structure needs base cases.

119
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:33,860
So that's a useful step not to forget.

120
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:35,440
We want to solve the original problem

121
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:36,440
using these subproblems.

122
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:38,900
And then we analyze our running time at the end.

123
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:42,040
So six easy steps.

124
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:44,020
Actually, the hardest ones are these two,

125
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:45,520
which are interrelated.

126
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:49,140
And what we're going to see over the next four lectures,

127
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:51,700
this is the first of four lectures on dynamic programming,

128
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:55,180
is lots of examples of applying this paradigm over and over

129
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:57,160
together with the memoization idea,

130
00:05:57,160 --> 00:05:58,960
which we'll get to soon.

131
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:03,080
Let's see an example first of an algorithm we've already seen,

132
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:07,480
which is merge sort, so a divide and conquer algorithm phrased

133
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:09,200
with this structure of sort bot.

134
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:12,640
So for the subproblems, so our original problem

135
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:16,000
is to sort the elements of A. And some subproblems

136
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,200
that we solve along the way are sorting

137
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:21,760
different subarrays of A. So for every, well,

138
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:24,460
not for every i and j, but for some i and j's,

139
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:27,160
we sort the items from i.

140
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:28,740
Up to j minus 1.

141
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:32,160
So I'm going to define that subproblem to be s of ij.

142
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:34,760
So this is something that I might want to solve.

143
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:37,160
The original problem that I want to solve

144
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:40,560
is s of 0 comma n, where n is the length of the array.

145
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:43,060
So that's what I actually care about in the end.

146
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:45,840
But we're going to solve that by writing it recursively

147
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:49,960
in terms of sorting different subarrays as follows.

148
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:51,840
This is the recurrence relation.

149
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,180
I've written it very simply here.

150
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,500
Of course, there's a merge algorithm, which

151
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:56,960
is somewhat complicated.

152
00:06:57,160 --> 00:07:00,720
But we saw the two-finger linear time merge algorithm

153
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:04,220
given two sorted arrays.

154
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:07,180
So this is supposed to be the sorted array version

155
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:08,940
of the items i through m.

156
00:07:08,940 --> 00:07:11,660
m is the middle element between i and j.

157
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:15,860
And the sorted array of the items from m up to j.

158
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:19,000
If we merge those, that gives us the sorted array

159
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,760
from i up to j.

160
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:24,320
And that's exactly what merge sort does.

161
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:26,760
So this is, in general, this relation,

162
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:29,800
which is the result of a simple algorithm.

163
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:32,099
And this is the result of a simple algorithm.

164
00:07:32,099 --> 00:07:33,860
And the result of a simple algorithm

165
00:07:33,860 --> 00:07:37,920
is just some algorithm for, if you're

166
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:40,700
given the solutions to some smaller subproblems,

167
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:47,140
how do I solve the subproblem that I want to solve?

168
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:51,640
And so we need to make sure that this problem is bigger

169
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,640
than the ones that we recursively call on

170
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:56,159
and that we don't get an infinite cyclic loop of

171
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:56,820
the array.

172
00:07:56,820 --> 00:07:58,240
And that's the way we can solve it.

173
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:00,520
So for example, if I want to solve the subproblem

174
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:03,080
that I want to solve, I'm going to have to do a little bit

175
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:20,620
of a

176
00:08:20,620 --> 00:08:24,040
We have a base case, which is if we don't want to sort anything,

177
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:25,080
that's the empty array.

178
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:26,000
And we have a subproblem.

179
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:26,100
And we have a base case.

180
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:26,120
And we have a base case.

181
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:26,140
And we have a base case.

182
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:27,560
I already said the original problem.

183
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:29,800
And then running time is, I mean,

184
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:32,860
there's no better way to solve it than the recurrence

185
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:34,520
that we already saw how to solve.

186
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:36,819
So this is just another way to think of n log n merge

187
00:08:36,819 --> 00:08:41,260
sort in this labeled framework of SortBot.

188
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:45,520
Let's get to another problem that does not

189
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:51,560
fit recursion so well, but we can make it better.

190
00:08:51,560 --> 00:08:54,700
So we're going to start with a very simple problem, which

191
00:08:54,700 --> 00:08:56,100
is computing Fibonacci.

192
00:08:56,100 --> 00:08:59,240
And we're going to use Fibonacci numbers.

193
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:02,100
It's really just a toy problem to illustrate

194
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:06,360
a very powerful idea, which is memorization.

195
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:08,540
So the problem I'm interested in is I'm

196
00:09:08,540 --> 00:09:10,980
given a particular number n.

197
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:14,820
And I want to compute the nth Fibonacci number.

198
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:17,460
And in case you forgot, the nth Fibonacci number

199
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:18,980
is given by this recurrence.

200
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:24,340
Fn is Fn minus 1 plus Fn minus 2 with base case, let's say,

201
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:26,060
F1 equals F2 equals F3.

202
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:26,719
Fn equals 1.

203
00:09:29,339 --> 00:09:31,719
And so we'd like to compute this.

204
00:09:31,719 --> 00:09:33,380
This is a recurrence.

205
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:36,260
So it seems very natural to write it

206
00:09:36,260 --> 00:09:37,540
as a recursive algorithm.

207
00:09:37,540 --> 00:09:38,719
So let's try to do it.

208
00:09:38,719 --> 00:09:41,619
We start with what are the subproblems?

209
00:09:41,619 --> 00:09:47,719
The obvious subproblems are just the various Fibonacci numbers.

210
00:09:47,719 --> 00:09:53,199
Fi for i between 1 and n.

211
00:09:53,199 --> 00:09:53,800
F1 equals 1.

212
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:54,300
Fn equals 1.

213
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:54,800
Fn equals 1.

214
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:55,300
Fn equals 1.

215
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:55,800
Fn equals 1.

216
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:56,060
Fn equals 1.

217
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:57,599
So there are n of these subproblems.

218
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:01,640
Cool.

219
00:10:01,640 --> 00:10:02,140
Let's see.

220
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:04,339
We want a relation between them.

221
00:10:07,699 --> 00:10:10,180
Well, maybe I'll just to distinguish the problems

222
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:13,780
from the Fibonacci numbers, let me write f of i.

223
00:10:13,780 --> 00:10:16,599
This is a function, an algorithm we're going to define.

224
00:10:16,599 --> 00:10:20,619
And it's defined to be the goal we're trying to get

225
00:10:20,619 --> 00:10:24,079
is the i Fibonacci number given i.

226
00:10:24,079 --> 00:10:25,800
And then we can write the recurrence.

227
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:28,199
Recurrence relation on these guys,

228
00:10:28,199 --> 00:10:34,199
just f of i equals f of i minus 1 plus f of i minus 2.

229
00:10:34,199 --> 00:10:38,020
So in other words, recursively compute those Fibonacci numbers,

230
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:38,979
then add them together.

231
00:10:38,979 --> 00:10:41,099
That's an algorithm.

232
00:10:41,099 --> 00:10:44,400
Next is t for topological order.

233
00:10:48,599 --> 00:10:51,979
Here, of course, we just want to compute these in order

234
00:10:51,979 --> 00:10:53,020
of increasing i.

235
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:55,339
From the basis.

236
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:58,020
From the base case is up.

237
00:10:58,020 --> 00:11:01,160
Another way I like to write this is as a for loop.

238
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:05,020
For i equals 1 to n.

239
00:11:05,020 --> 00:11:06,840
We'll see why.

240
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:13,560
But this gives an explicit order to compute these subproblems.

241
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:21,720
And base case is just the same as the Fibonacci numbers.

242
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:23,340
But I guess I should write parentheses.

243
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:31,020
So the original problem we want to solve is f of n.

244
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:33,220
And the time, all right, here's where

245
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:35,020
things get interesting or bad.

246
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:39,720
So what is the running time of this recursive algorithm?

247
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:42,580
As I've stated it so far, the running time

248
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:46,020
is given by a recurrence.

249
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:47,780
Let's write the recurrence.

250
00:11:47,780 --> 00:11:54,020
So in order to compute f of n, I recursively compute f of i minus 1, or f of i minus 1.

251
00:11:54,020 --> 00:11:55,060
So I'm going to compute the recursive algorithm.

252
00:11:55,060 --> 00:11:58,599
So I'm going to compute the recursive algorithm for f of n minus 1 here.

253
00:11:58,599 --> 00:12:03,839
And I recursively compute f of n minus 2.

254
00:12:03,839 --> 00:12:05,579
So that will take t of n minus 2.

255
00:12:05,579 --> 00:12:08,020
This first step will take t of n minus 1.

256
00:12:08,020 --> 00:12:11,479
And now I need to solve this recurrence.

257
00:12:11,479 --> 00:12:14,459
This is not a recurrence that falls to the master method.

258
00:12:14,459 --> 00:12:17,800
It doesn't have a divided by.

259
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:19,640
So we have to think about it a little bit.

