00:23:16,879 --> 00:23:23,680
So I'm referring to subproblems, the number of them,

484
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:26,760
and the time it takes to evaluate this,

485
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:28,379
ignoring the recursive calls.

486
00:23:28,379 --> 00:23:29,100
That's important.

487
00:23:29,100 --> 00:23:33,640
This is the non-recursive part.

488
00:23:33,640 --> 00:23:40,940
In the notes, I call this non-recursive work.

489
00:23:40,940 --> 00:23:41,440
OK.

490
00:23:41,440 --> 00:23:41,980
So we have n subproblems.

491
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:42,379
And we have n subproblems.

492
00:23:42,379 --> 00:23:42,879
So we have n subproblems.

493
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:50,760
So this formula gives us a way to bound the running time

494
00:23:50,760 --> 00:23:54,220
of one of these algorithms if we use memoization.

495
00:23:54,220 --> 00:23:55,840
Without memoization, this is not true.

496
00:23:55,840 --> 00:23:57,820
Fibonacci took exponential time.

497
00:23:57,820 --> 00:23:59,600
But if we add memoization, we know

498
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:01,640
that we only solve each subproblem once.

499
00:24:01,640 --> 00:24:03,740
And so we just need to see, for each one,

500
00:24:03,740 --> 00:24:05,580
how much did it cost me to compute it,

501
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:07,440
assuming all the recursion work is free,

502
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:11,080
because that's already taken into account by the summation.

503
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:12,860
So in particular, this summation is, at most,

504
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:16,000
the number of subproblems times the time per subproblem, which

505
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:17,600
in this case is order n.

