at most n squared different choices.

523
00:25:11,580 --> 00:25:12,720
In fact, it's the triangular number.

524
00:25:12,720 --> 00:25:19,260
The sum of i equals 1 to n of i, different possible choices

525
00:25:19,260 --> 00:25:20,380
for i and j.

526
00:25:20,380 --> 00:25:25,200
But this is theta n squared subproblems,

527
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:27,480
which seems not so good.

528
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:30,020
And then how much time are we spending per subproblem?

529
00:25:30,020 --> 00:25:33,900
Well, to solve s of ij, we have to merge

530
00:25:33,900 --> 00:25:35,220
about that many elements.

531
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:36,920
We know merge takes linear time.

532
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:41,480
And so this takes theta j minus i time to evaluate.

533
00:25:42,720 --> 00:25:47,280
And so what we'd like to do is sum over all the subproblems

534
00:25:47,280 --> 00:25:48,240
of j minus i.

535
00:25:48,240 --> 00:25:51,220
This is the not triangular number,

536
00:25:51,220 --> 00:25:53,840
but the tetrahedral number, I guess.

537
00:25:53,840 --> 00:25:57,500
And so we end up that the running time is at most

538
00:25:57,500 --> 00:25:58,080
n cubed.

539
00:26:00,800 --> 00:26:02,100
Great.

540
00:26:02,100 --> 00:26:06,020
So it's true that n log n is less than or equal to n cubed,

541
00:26:06,020 --> 00:26:07,740
but obviously not terribly useful.

542
00:26:07,740 --> 00:26:11,280
This algorithm, by the way we already know how to analyze it,

543
00:26:11,280 --> 00:26:12,560
is indeed n log n.

544
00:26:12,560 --> 00:26:17,120
And the running time turns out to be theta n log n.

545
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:20,960
So sometimes this equation is not what you want to use.

546
00:26:20,960 --> 00:26:22,379
But often it's good enough.

547
00:26:22,379 --> 00:26:25,080
And especially if you just want to get a polynomial upper bound,

548
00:26:25,080 --> 00:26:27,300
and then you can try to optimize it later.

549
00:26:27,300 --> 00:26:29,020
This will give you a polynomial upper bound

550
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:31,220
as long as the number of subproblems is polynomial

551
00:26:31,220 --> 00:26:34,200
and the time per subproblem is polynomial.

552
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:35,899
And indeed, n cubed is polynomial.

553
00:26:35,899 --> 00:26:39,620
It's not a great polynomial, but this is an alternate way

554
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:41,120
to analyze merge sort.

555
00:26:41,120 --> 00:26:42,399
Obviously, don't do this.

556
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:47,980
It's not super merge sort, but it illustrates the technique.

557
00:26:47,980 --> 00:26:49,360
Good so far?

558
00:26:49,360 --> 00:26:51,380
Any questions?

559
00:26:51,380 --> 00:26:52,880
All right.

560
00:26:52,880 --> 00:26:55,300
Let me remember where we are.

561
00:26:57,780 --> 00:26:58,280
Cool.

562
00:26:58,280 --> 00:27:01,620
So the next thing I'd like to do is show you

563
00:27:01,620 --> 00:27:04,080
one more algorithm that we've already seen in this class that

564
00:27:04,080 --> 00:27:08,080
fits very nicely into this structure, arguably

565
00:27:08,080 --> 00:27:09,700
is a dynamic program.

566
00:27:09,700 --> 00:27:12,220
And that is DAG shortest path.

567
00:27:12,400 --> 00:27:15,660
Oh, so just to close the loop here,

568
00:27:15,660 --> 00:27:17,540
when I say dynamic programming, I

569
00:27:17,540 --> 00:27:22,020
mean recursion with memoization.

570
00:27:22,020 --> 00:27:28,520
I mean we write a recursive piece of code, which

571
00:27:28,520 --> 00:27:38,200
is like def f of some args, some subproblem specification.

572
00:27:38,200 --> 00:27:39,920
We check is the subproblem.

573
00:27:42,400 --> 00:27:52,000
In the memo table, if so, return memo of subproblem.

574
00:27:56,560 --> 00:28:01,540
And otherwise, check if it's a base case

575
00:28:01,540 --> 00:28:03,100
and solve it if it's a base case.

576
00:28:03,100 --> 00:28:08,880
And otherwise, write the recurrence,

577
00:28:08,880 --> 00:28:11,360
recurse via relation.

578
00:28:12,400 --> 00:28:20,700
And set the memo table of the subproblem

579
00:28:20,700 --> 00:28:23,620
to be one of those things.

580
00:28:23,620 --> 00:28:26,560
So this is the generic dynamic program.

581
00:28:26,560 --> 00:28:32,160
And implicitly, I'm writing Fibonacci in that way.

582
00:28:32,160 --> 00:28:35,460
And all of the dynamic programs have this implicit structure,

583
00:28:35,460 --> 00:28:38,140
where I start with a memo table, which is empty.

584
00:28:40,600 --> 00:28:41,340
And I open it.

585
00:28:41,340 --> 00:28:42,300
And I want to write a code.

586
00:28:42,300 --> 00:28:44,500
I always just check if I'm in the memo table.

587
00:28:44,500 --> 00:28:45,540
If I am, I return it.

588
00:28:45,540 --> 00:28:50,639
Otherwise, I compute according to this recursive relation

589
00:28:50,639 --> 00:28:55,300
by recursively calling F. And that's it.

590
00:28:55,300 --> 00:29:00,520
So this is every DP algorithm is going to have that structure.

591
00:29:00,520 --> 00:29:05,200
And it's just using recursion and memoization together.

592
00:29:05,200 --> 00:29:07,000
So now let's apply that technique

593
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:10,120
to think about the DAG shortest paths problem.

594
00:29:10,120 --> 00:29:10,500
The problem was I give you.

595
00:29:10,500 --> 00:29:11,000
DAG shortest paths.

596
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:11,500
DAG shortest paths.

597
00:29:11,500 --> 00:29:12,000
DAG shortest paths.

598
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:12,880
So I give you a DAG.

599
00:29:12,880 --> 00:29:16,560
I give you a source vertex, S. Single source shortest paths.

600
00:29:16,560 --> 00:29:20,560
Compute the shortest path weight from S to every vertex.

601
00:29:20,560 --> 00:29:22,440
That's the goal of the problem.

602
00:29:22,440 --> 00:29:24,840
And we saw a way to solve that, which is DAG relaxation.

603
00:29:24,840 --> 00:29:26,660
I'm going to show you a different way, which

604
00:29:26,660 --> 00:29:29,920
turns out to be basically the same, but upside down

605
00:29:29,920 --> 00:29:33,000
or flipped left to right, depending

606
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,700
which way you direct your edges.

607
00:29:35,700 --> 00:29:38,160
So what are our subproblems?

608
00:29:38,160 --> 00:29:40,540
Well, here, actually, they're kind of spelled out for us.

609
00:29:40,540 --> 00:29:41,700
We want to compute delta of SV.

610
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:47,280
for all v. So that is size of v subproblems.

611
00:29:47,280 --> 00:29:51,940
That turns out to be enough for this overall problem.

612
00:29:51,940 --> 00:29:53,660
And the original problem we want to solve

613
00:29:53,660 --> 00:29:55,280
is all of the subproblems.

614
00:29:55,280 --> 00:29:57,080
We solve all the subproblems, we're done.

615
00:29:57,080 --> 00:30:00,760
And then we have, I think we wrote this at some point

616
00:30:00,760 --> 00:30:03,720
during the DAG shortest paths lecture,

617
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:07,520
we have a recursive relation saying that the shortest way

618
00:30:07,520 --> 00:30:12,500
to get from s to v is the minimum of the shortest path

619
00:30:12,500 --> 00:30:16,300
to get to some vertex u plus the weight of the edge from u to v.

620
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:17,000
Why?

621
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:21,240
Because if we look at a vertex v, unless we started there,

622
00:30:21,240 --> 00:30:23,420
we came from somewhere.

623
00:30:23,420 --> 00:30:27,800
And so we can consider all of the possible choices

624
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:30,120
for the previous vertex u.

625
00:30:30,120 --> 00:30:33,040
And if you start at s and get to v,

626
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:35,080
you must go through one of them.

627
00:30:35,080 --> 00:30:37,100
And so this is finding the best way

628
00:30:37,100 --> 00:30:37,500
among all of the subproblems.

629
00:30:37,500 --> 00:30:40,720
So all the choices of u, what's the best way to get to u,

630
00:30:40,720 --> 00:30:44,500
and then take the edge from u to v for all edges uv.

631
00:30:44,500 --> 00:30:46,760
And this is adjacency minus.

632
00:30:46,760 --> 00:30:48,500
We don't usually think of that.

633
00:30:48,500 --> 00:30:51,040
Usually we look at adjacency plus, the outgoing edges.

634
00:30:51,040 --> 00:30:53,300
This is the incoming edges.

635
00:30:53,300 --> 00:30:58,900
And so uv is an incoming edge into v.

636
00:30:58,900 --> 00:31:00,900
If we take that minimum, and of course,

637
00:31:00,900 --> 00:31:03,320
it's possible there is no way to get to v.

638
00:31:03,320 --> 00:31:06,080
And so I'll also throw infinity into this set.

639
00:31:06,080 --> 00:31:07,280
Take the min of that set.

640
00:31:07,280 --> 00:31:10,879
That will give me the shortest pathway in an acyclic graph

641
00:31:10,879 --> 00:31:12,639
from s to v. And great.

642
00:31:12,639 --> 00:31:13,800
This is recursive.

643
00:31:13,800 --> 00:31:15,040
This was a subproblem.

644
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:19,700
These are subproblems which are smaller, I guess.

645
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:22,639
There's no clear notion of smaller here,

646
00:31:22,639 --> 00:31:25,500
except we already know the clear notion of smaller

647
00:31:25,500 --> 00:31:30,700
is the topological order of our DAG.

648
00:31:30,700 --> 00:31:32,240
Because our graph is acyclic, we know

649
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:33,360
it has a topological order.

650
00:31:33,360 --> 00:31:36,440
We know how to compute it with DFS.

651
00:31:36,440 --> 00:31:37,080
And so that guarantees that we're going to get to v.

652
00:31:37,080 --> 00:31:37,120
And so we're going to get to v.

653
00:31:37,120 --> 00:31:37,160
And so we're going to get to v.

654
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:37,260
And so we're going to get to v.

655
00:31:37,280 --> 00:31:38,639
And so we're going to get to v. And in that guarantees,

656
00:31:38,639 --> 00:31:41,940
there is a topological order to compute these problems.

657
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:47,080
And in fact, the relationship between problems

658
00:31:47,080 --> 00:31:50,560
is exactly the given graph g.

659
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:54,480
In order to compute the shortest path weight from s to v,

660
00:31:54,480 --> 00:31:56,680
I need to know the shortest path weight from s to all

661
00:31:56,680 --> 00:31:58,700
of the incoming vertices to v.

662
00:31:58,700 --> 00:32:02,260
And so this is, I guess, in the call graph,

663
00:32:02,260 --> 00:32:05,680
this vertex calls this vertex.

664
00:32:05,680 --> 00:32:07,160
But I'll direct the edge.

665
00:32:07,160 --> 00:32:11,980
I'll direct the edge this way to say that this vertex requires

666
00:32:11,980 --> 00:32:14,920
this vertex needs to be computed before this one.

667
00:32:14,920 --> 00:32:19,480
And so then I can compute them in a topological order.

668
00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:22,840
We have a base case, which is delta of ss equals 0.

669
00:32:22,840 --> 00:32:26,980
And the running time is, again, we can use this formula

670
00:32:26,980 --> 00:32:29,280
and say, let's just sum over all the subproblems

671
00:32:29,280 --> 00:32:32,400
of the non-recursive work in our recurrence relation.

672
00:32:32,400 --> 00:32:34,780
And so it's computing this min.

673
00:32:34,780 --> 00:32:37,040
If I gave you these delta of ss equals 0,

674
00:32:37,040 --> 00:32:39,800
and I gave you these deltas for free,

675
00:32:39,800 --> 00:32:42,620
and I gave you these weights, which we know from our weight

676
00:32:42,620 --> 00:32:45,420
data structure, how long does it take to compute this min?

677
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:47,159
Well, however many things there are,

678
00:32:47,159 --> 00:32:49,360
how many numbers we're minning, which

679
00:32:49,360 --> 00:32:52,860
is the size of the incoming adjacency list,

680
00:32:52,860 --> 00:32:55,420
plus 1 for that infinity.

681
00:32:55,420 --> 00:32:58,460
And so if you compute this sum, sum of incoming edges

682
00:32:58,460 --> 00:33:01,159
to every vertex, that's all the edges.

683
00:33:01,159 --> 00:33:03,920
So this is v plus e.

684
00:33:03,920 --> 00:33:04,420
OK?

685
00:33:04,420 --> 00:33:07,040
So in fact, this algorithm, which

686
00:33:07,040 --> 00:33:10,020
is morally the same algorithm as the one

687
00:33:10,020 --> 00:33:12,320
that we saw in the DAG shortest path lecture, which

688
00:33:12,320 --> 00:33:18,680
was compute a topological order and process vertices

689
00:33:18,680 --> 00:33:23,800
in that order and relax edges going out from vertices.

690
00:33:23,800 --> 00:33:26,960
So here, so in that algorithm, we

691
00:33:26,960 --> 00:33:29,120
would have tried to relax this edge

692
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:31,340
if there was a better path to v.

693
00:33:31,340 --> 00:33:33,659
The first one certainly is better than infinity.

694
00:33:33,659 --> 00:33:36,840
So the first one, we relax indeed.

695
00:33:36,840 --> 00:33:40,000
Now the next edge, if this gave a better path from s to v,

696
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:42,520
then we would relax that edge and update the weight here

697
00:33:42,520 --> 00:33:43,740
and do the same here.

698
00:33:43,740 --> 00:33:46,959
In the end, we're just computing this min in the relaxation

699
00:33:46,959 --> 00:33:48,580
algorithm, but doing it step by step.

700
00:33:48,580 --> 00:33:52,480
In the relaxation algorithm, DAG relaxation,

701
00:33:52,480 --> 00:33:58,899
for each incoming edge to v, we update d of v if it's better.

702
00:33:58,899 --> 00:34:02,080
And so if you repeatedly update if you're better,

703
00:34:02,080 --> 00:34:04,280
that ends up computing a min.

704
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:04,780
OK?

705
00:34:04,780 --> 00:34:06,820
So this is the same algorithm, just kind

706
00:34:06,840 --> 00:34:09,060
of flipped backwards.

707
00:34:09,060 --> 00:34:11,279
A funny thing, although we wrote down

708
00:34:11,279 --> 00:34:15,600
the topological order of the subproblem graph here

709
00:34:15,600 --> 00:34:19,960
is the topological order of g, because the subproblem graph is

710
00:34:19,960 --> 00:34:24,719
g, the algorithm doesn't actually have to compute one.

711
00:34:24,719 --> 00:34:27,199
It's doing it automatically for free.

712
00:34:27,199 --> 00:34:32,299
If you think about this algorithm, generic dp

713
00:34:32,299 --> 00:34:35,659
algorithm, which is check whether we're in our memo

714
00:34:35,659 --> 00:34:36,159
table.

715
00:34:36,159 --> 00:34:36,580
If so, return.

716
00:34:36,580 --> 00:34:40,900
Otherwise, recurse, or base case.

717
00:34:40,900 --> 00:34:43,299
This actually is a depth-first search

718
00:34:43,299 --> 00:34:45,460
through the subproblem graph, technically

719
00:34:45,460 --> 00:34:48,460
through the reverse of the subproblem graph.

720
00:34:48,460 --> 00:34:58,420
If I draw an edge, so from small to big,

721
00:34:58,420 --> 00:35:02,140
so I'm just saying I orient the edges from my smaller

722
00:35:02,140 --> 00:35:04,380
subproblems to the ones that need it,

723
00:35:04,380 --> 00:35:05,880
then I'm actually depth-first searching back

724
00:35:05,880 --> 00:35:06,380
to the problem.

725
00:35:06,380 --> 00:35:06,580
OK?

726
00:35:06,580 --> 00:35:08,580
So I'm going to do a little bit of a back-and-forth

727
00:35:08,580 --> 00:35:11,200
in this graph, because the bigger problem calls

728
00:35:11,200 --> 00:35:13,540
the smaller problem.

729
00:35:13,540 --> 00:35:16,900
And the memo table is serving as the have I visited

730
00:35:16,900 --> 00:35:19,720
this vertex already check in DFS.

731
00:35:19,720 --> 00:35:21,880
So this is actually a DFS algorithm,

732
00:35:21,880 --> 00:35:25,000
plus we're doing some computation

733
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:28,340
to actually solve the subproblems we care about.

734
00:35:28,340 --> 00:35:31,180
So implicit in this algorithm, we are doing a DFS.

735
00:35:31,180 --> 00:35:33,940
And at the same time, we're doing this shortest path

736
00:35:33,940 --> 00:35:35,200
computation.

737
00:35:35,200 --> 00:35:35,700
OK?

738
00:35:35,700 --> 00:35:38,080
So this is the finishing order of that DFS traversal.

739
00:35:38,080 --> 00:35:39,500
Because all the edges are backwards,

740
00:35:39,500 --> 00:35:41,740
this is the same as the reverse finishing order

741
00:35:41,740 --> 00:35:42,819
if the graph is forward.

742
00:35:42,819 --> 00:35:46,580
So in the end, we're computing a topological order

743
00:35:46,580 --> 00:35:51,399
because dynamic programming includes in it depth first

744
00:35:51,399 --> 00:35:51,899
search.

745
00:35:51,899 --> 00:35:54,720
Whew, a lot of words.

746
00:35:54,720 --> 00:35:58,140
But it's kind of cool that this framework just

747
00:35:58,140 --> 00:36:02,399
solves DAG shortest paths without much work.

748
00:36:02,399 --> 00:36:04,319
I mean, we did a lot of work in shortest paths

749
00:36:04,319 --> 00:36:05,500
to prove that this relation is true.

750
00:36:05,500 --> 00:36:08,420
But once you know it's true, the algorithm part

751
00:36:08,420 --> 00:36:11,400
is pretty much free.

752
00:36:11,400 --> 00:36:16,060
You just write down sort bot, and you're done.

753
00:36:16,060 --> 00:36:18,560
OK.

754
00:36:18,560 --> 00:36:24,780
This brings us to, in general, at this point,

755
00:36:24,780 --> 00:36:27,320
we have seen two examples of dynamic programming.

756
00:36:27,320 --> 00:36:28,900
I guess technically merge sort you

757
00:36:28,900 --> 00:36:30,200
could think of as a dynamic program,

758
00:36:30,200 --> 00:36:31,560
but it doesn't actually reuse anything.

759
00:36:31,560 --> 00:36:32,760
So it's not interesting.

760
00:36:32,760 --> 00:36:34,740
And indeed, that gave us a really bad bound.

761
00:36:34,740 --> 00:36:37,299
But we've definitely seen DAG shortest paths and Fibonacci

762
00:36:37,299 --> 00:36:39,640
numbers as two interesting examples.

763
00:36:39,640 --> 00:36:42,359
And what the next remainder of this lecture

764
00:36:42,359 --> 00:36:44,779
and the next three lectures are going to be about

765
00:36:44,779 --> 00:36:48,180
is more and more examples of dynamic programming

766
00:36:48,180 --> 00:36:51,459
and how you can use it to solve increasingly general problems.

767
00:36:51,459 --> 00:36:53,839
So far, we've just solved an easy problem and a problem

768
00:36:53,839 --> 00:36:55,240
we already knew how to solve.

769
00:36:55,240 --> 00:37:02,839
Let's go to a new problem, which is bowling.

770
00:37:02,839 --> 00:37:04,180
Bowling is popular in Boston.

771
00:37:04,740 --> 00:37:12,100
Boston likes to play candle pin bowling, which is a bit unusual.

772
00:37:12,100 --> 00:37:15,779
Today, we're going to play an even more unusual bowling game,

773
00:37:15,779 --> 00:37:20,299
one that I made up based on a bowling game that Henry

774
00:37:20,299 --> 00:37:22,859
Doudny made up in 1908.

775
00:37:22,859 --> 00:37:26,259
So ancient bowling, you might call it.

776
00:37:26,259 --> 00:37:29,140
Or I think linear bowling is what I might call it.

777
00:37:29,140 --> 00:37:32,060
I'll just call it bowling here.

778
00:37:32,060 --> 00:37:34,339
And now I'm going to attempt to draw a bowling pin.

779
00:37:34,339 --> 00:37:34,740
Not a bowling pin.

780
00:37:34,740 --> 00:37:37,080
Not bad.

781
00:37:37,080 --> 00:37:39,520
That might get progressively worse.

782
00:37:39,520 --> 00:37:42,339
So imagine n identical bowling pins.

