786
00:37:50,180 --> 00:37:52,419
These bowling pins are pretty close together.

787
00:37:52,419 --> 00:37:54,879
I should have left a little gap here.

788
00:37:54,879 --> 00:37:57,399
And you are a really good bowler.

789
00:37:57,399 --> 00:38:00,939
Now, unfortunately, these bowling pins are on a line.

790
00:38:00,939 --> 00:38:03,939
And you're bowling from way down at infinity.

791
00:38:04,740 --> 00:38:09,680
So when you bowl, you can only hit one pin or two pins,

792
00:38:09,680 --> 00:38:10,620
or zero pins.

793
00:38:10,620 --> 00:38:13,060
But probably you want to hit some pins.

794
00:38:13,060 --> 00:38:17,140
So if you bowl straight at a pin, you will just hit that one pin.

795
00:38:17,140 --> 00:38:20,839
And if you bowl in the middle between two pins,

796
00:38:20,839 --> 00:38:26,200
you will knock down two pins.

797
00:38:26,200 --> 00:38:31,040
This is your model of bowling, model of computation.

798
00:38:31,040 --> 00:38:34,120
Now, what makes this interesting is that the pins have values.

799
00:38:34,120 --> 00:38:39,960
Pin i has value vi.

800
00:38:39,960 --> 00:38:42,799
This is obviously a toy problem.

801
00:38:42,799 --> 00:38:47,660
Though this problem, this type of bowling, does go back to 1908.

802
00:38:47,660 --> 00:38:50,620
It was also a toy problem in that setting.

803
00:38:50,620 --> 00:38:55,680
So each of these bowling pins has some number on it.

804
00:38:55,680 --> 00:39:00,620
Let's say 199.

805
00:39:00,620 --> 00:39:03,560
Let me do a slightly more interesting example, maybe

806
00:39:03,560 --> 00:39:03,720
another 1.

807
00:39:03,720 --> 00:39:05,879
Here.

808
00:39:05,879 --> 00:39:11,680
And a 2, and a 5, and a 5, something like this.

