00:41:07,840 --> 00:41:09,480
minus 5 times minus 5 is good.

855
00:41:09,480 --> 00:41:11,300
That's 25 points.

856
00:41:11,300 --> 00:41:14,820
So the optimal solution for this particular instance

857
00:41:14,820 --> 00:41:19,140
are to hit all the pins, these positive, these together,

858
00:41:19,140 --> 00:41:20,080
these together.

859
00:41:20,080 --> 00:41:23,460
If I added, for example, another pin of minus 3 here,

860
00:41:23,460 --> 00:41:25,700
I would choose not to hit that pin.

861
00:41:25,700 --> 00:41:26,400
Good question.

862
00:41:26,400 --> 00:41:29,460
So you just play until you are tired.

863
00:41:29,460 --> 00:41:32,760
When you decide to stop playing, how can I maximize your score?

864
00:41:32,760 --> 00:41:34,800
There are many variations in this game.

865
00:41:34,800 --> 00:41:38,620
All of them, basically any variation, not literally

866
00:41:38,620 --> 00:41:41,160
every variation, but many, many variations of this problem

867
00:41:41,160 --> 00:41:44,580
can all be solved quickly with dynamic programming.

868
00:41:44,580 --> 00:41:48,120
But let's solve this particular one.

869
00:41:48,120 --> 00:41:48,620
OK.

870
00:41:55,120 --> 00:41:58,620
So now we're really in algorithmic design mode.

871
00:41:58,620 --> 00:42:01,760
We need to think about sort bot.

872
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:02,760
And in particular, we need to think

873
00:42:02,760 --> 00:42:06,080
about what would the subproblems be here.

874
00:42:06,080 --> 00:42:08,540
And at this point, we don't have a lot of help.

875
00:42:08,540 --> 00:42:11,460
So I should probably give you some tools.

876
00:42:11,460 --> 00:42:13,440
If I want to solve a problem like this,

877
00:42:13,440 --> 00:42:18,140
the input is a sequence of numbers.

878
00:42:18,140 --> 00:42:19,800
It's a sequence data structure.

879
00:42:19,800 --> 00:42:23,920
Maybe it's an array of numbers, which is this v array.

880
00:42:26,760 --> 00:42:28,960
And let's see.

881
00:42:28,960 --> 00:42:39,720
A general tool for subproblem design,

882
00:42:39,720 --> 00:42:42,020
which will cover most of the problems, maybe

883
00:42:42,020 --> 00:42:45,140
all of the problems that we see in this class

884
00:42:45,140 --> 00:42:47,140
for dynamic programming.

885
00:42:47,140 --> 00:42:49,780
Here's a trick.

886
00:42:49,780 --> 00:42:52,960
If your input is a sequence, you can't solve it.

887
00:42:52,960 --> 00:42:53,960
You can't solve it.

888
00:42:53,960 --> 00:42:54,960
You can't solve it.

889
00:42:54,960 --> 00:42:55,960
You can't solve it.

890
00:42:55,960 --> 00:42:56,960
You can't solve it.

891
00:42:56,960 --> 00:42:57,960
You can't solve it.

892
00:42:57,960 --> 00:43:01,200
You can't solve it.

893
00:43:01,200 --> 00:43:04,340
Here are some good subproblems to consider.

894
00:43:13,160 --> 00:43:15,640
We could do all prefixes.

895
00:43:18,200 --> 00:43:22,340
So let's call the sequence x.

896
00:43:22,340 --> 00:43:27,720
So we could do x prefix means up to a given i for all i.

897
00:43:27,720 --> 00:43:35,000
We could do all the suffixes, x from i onward for all i.

898
00:43:35,000 --> 00:43:41,359
Or we could do substrings, which are the consecutive items

899
00:43:41,359 --> 00:43:43,319
from i to j.

900
00:43:43,319 --> 00:43:44,560
I don't write subsequence here.

901
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:47,220
Subsequence means you can omit items in the middle.

902
00:43:47,220 --> 00:43:49,180
So substring, you have to start at some position

903
00:43:49,180 --> 00:43:52,040
and do all the things up to j.

904
00:43:52,040 --> 00:43:55,339
So these are nice, easy to express in Python notation.

905
00:43:55,339 --> 00:43:57,000
And these are great because they're polynomial.

906
00:43:57,000 --> 00:43:58,000
They're polynomial.

907
00:43:58,000 --> 00:44:02,240
If I have n things, if the length of my sequence x is n,

908
00:44:02,240 --> 00:44:06,060
then there are n sequences, n prefixes, technically n plus 1.

909
00:44:06,060 --> 00:44:08,640
So let's do theta n prefixes.

910
00:44:08,640 --> 00:44:10,380
There are theta n suffixes.

911
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:14,360
And there are theta n squared substrings

912
00:44:14,360 --> 00:44:19,200
because there's roughly n choices for i and j separately.

913
00:44:19,200 --> 00:44:19,700
Sorry?

914
00:44:19,700 --> 00:44:21,140
What about subsequences?

915
00:44:21,140 --> 00:44:22,280
Subsequences.

916
00:44:22,280 --> 00:44:22,780
Good.

917
00:44:22,780 --> 00:44:24,820
I didn't write subsequences because, in fact, there

918
00:44:24,820 --> 00:44:26,240
are exponentially many subsequences.

919
00:44:26,240 --> 00:44:26,740
Right?

920
00:44:26,740 --> 00:44:27,819
It's 2 to the n.

921
00:44:27,819 --> 00:44:30,140
For every item, I can choose it or not.

922
00:44:30,140 --> 00:44:31,959
So I don't want to parametrize.

923
00:44:31,959 --> 00:44:34,240
I don't want my subproblems to be subsequences

924
00:44:34,240 --> 00:44:35,919
because that's guaranteed.

925
00:44:35,919 --> 00:44:37,660
Well, then you're guaranteed to get

926
00:44:37,660 --> 00:44:40,339
an exponential number of subproblems, which is bad.

927
00:44:40,339 --> 00:44:43,720
We'd like to bound the number of subproblems by a polynomial.

928
00:44:43,720 --> 00:44:47,859
So these are three natural ways to get polynomial bounds.

929
00:44:47,859 --> 00:44:50,140
Now, prefixes and suffixes are obviously better

930
00:44:50,140 --> 00:44:53,299
because there's fewer of them, linear instead of quadratic.

931
00:44:53,299 --> 00:44:56,720
And usually, almost every problem you'll encounter

932
00:44:56,740 --> 00:44:58,359
is going to be a problem.

933
00:44:58,359 --> 00:45:00,640
So prefixes and suffixes are equally good.

934
00:45:00,640 --> 00:45:02,799
It doesn't really matter which one you choose.

935
00:45:02,799 --> 00:45:06,359
So maybe you like to think of, well, we'll get to.

936
00:45:08,359 --> 00:45:12,819
Just choose whichever is more comfortable for you.

937
00:45:12,819 --> 00:45:14,439
But sometimes it's not enough, and we'll

938
00:45:14,439 --> 00:45:15,439
have to go to substrings.

939
00:45:15,439 --> 00:45:17,740
That won't be for another lecture or two.

940
00:45:17,740 --> 00:45:18,240
OK.

941
00:45:18,240 --> 00:45:23,740
Today, I claim that prefixes or suffixes are enough

942
00:45:23,740 --> 00:45:25,819
to solve the bowling problem.

943
00:45:25,820 --> 00:45:29,320
So what we're going to do is think about, I prefer suffixes

944
00:45:29,320 --> 00:45:31,880
usually because I like to work from left to right,

945
00:45:31,880 --> 00:45:33,920
from the beginning to the end.

946
00:45:33,920 --> 00:45:38,580
So we're going to think of a suffix of the bowling pins.

947
00:45:38,580 --> 00:45:40,880
And so what is the subproblem on a suffix?

948
00:45:40,880 --> 00:45:43,160
Well, a natural version is just to solve

949
00:45:43,160 --> 00:45:45,260
the original problem, bowling.

950
00:45:45,260 --> 00:45:47,780
How do I maximize my score if all I were given

951
00:45:47,780 --> 00:45:48,860
were these pins?

952
00:45:48,860 --> 00:45:52,160
Suppose the pins to the left of i didn't exist.

953
00:45:52,160 --> 00:45:54,580
How would I maximize my score on the remaining pins?

954
00:45:54,580 --> 00:45:55,400
Or for this suffix?

955
00:45:55,400 --> 00:45:56,900
If I had four pins, what would I do?

956
00:45:56,900 --> 00:45:58,280
And there's some weird subproblems here, right?

957
00:45:58,280 --> 00:46:00,620
If I just gave you the last pin, what would you do?

958
00:46:00,620 --> 00:46:01,180
Nothing.

959
00:46:01,180 --> 00:46:04,440
That's clearly different from what I would do globally here.

960
00:46:04,440 --> 00:46:06,520
But I claim if I can solve all suffixes,

961
00:46:06,520 --> 00:46:09,760
I can solve my original problem because one of the suffixes

962
00:46:09,760 --> 00:46:11,580
is the whole sequence.

963
00:46:14,180 --> 00:46:15,160
So let's do it.

964
00:46:18,980 --> 00:46:22,820
Sort bot for bowling.

965
00:46:25,400 --> 00:46:29,220
So here is our dynamic program.

966
00:46:29,220 --> 00:46:34,080
The subproblems are suffixes.

967
00:46:34,080 --> 00:46:40,020
So I'll write b of i is the maximum score we could get.

968
00:46:43,060 --> 00:46:50,520
Possible with our starting.

969
00:46:50,520 --> 00:46:52,180
If we started a game with pins i, I, and i, if I started a game

970
00:46:52,180 --> 00:46:54,060
with pins i, I would get a maximum score.

971
00:46:54,060 --> 00:46:54,780
So for example, if I started a game with pins i, I would get

972
00:46:54,780 --> 00:46:55,180
a maximum score of a pin.

973
00:46:55,180 --> 00:47:02,560
i plus 1 up to n minus 1, which is a suffix of the pins.

974
00:47:02,560 --> 00:47:04,600
Very important whenever you write a dynamic program

975
00:47:04,600 --> 00:47:06,640
to define what your subproblems are.

976
00:47:06,640 --> 00:47:08,200
Don't just say how to compute them,

977
00:47:08,200 --> 00:47:10,779
but first say what is the goal of the subproblem.

978
00:47:10,779 --> 00:47:13,339
This is a common mistake to forget to state

979
00:47:13,339 --> 00:47:15,540
what you're trying to do.

980
00:47:15,540 --> 00:47:18,940
So now I've defined b of i.

981
00:47:18,940 --> 00:47:23,080
Now what is the original thing I'm trying to solve?

982
00:47:23,080 --> 00:47:26,040
You can also put in sort bot, you could put the o earlier.

983
00:47:26,040 --> 00:47:28,120
Then it actually spells sort.

984
00:47:28,120 --> 00:47:30,100
So why don't I do that for fun?

985
00:47:30,100 --> 00:47:32,440
The original problem we're trying to solve

986
00:47:32,440 --> 00:47:37,060
is b of 0, because that is all of the pins.

987
00:47:37,060 --> 00:47:40,000
The suffix starting at 0 is everything.

988
00:47:40,000 --> 00:47:42,700
So if we can solve that, we're done.

989
00:47:42,700 --> 00:47:46,140
Next is r for relate.

990
00:47:46,140 --> 00:47:49,420
This is the test of did I get the subproblems right,

991
00:47:49,420 --> 00:47:53,020
is whether I can write a recurrence relation.

992
00:47:53,020 --> 00:47:53,060
So.

993
00:47:53,060 --> 00:47:54,500
So let's try to do it.

994
00:47:54,500 --> 00:47:58,420
We want to compute b of i.

995
00:47:58,420 --> 00:48:04,080
So we have pin i here, and then the remaining pins.

996
00:48:07,740 --> 00:48:10,860
And the big idea here is to just think about,

997
00:48:10,860 --> 00:48:13,620
the nice thing about suffixes is if I take off

998
00:48:13,620 --> 00:48:16,180
something from the beginning, I still have a suffix.

999
00:48:16,180 --> 00:48:18,220
Remember, my goal is to take this subproblem, which

1000
00:48:18,220 --> 00:48:20,320
is the suffix starting at i, and reduce it

1001
00:48:20,320 --> 00:48:23,000
to a smaller subproblem, which means a smaller suffix.

1002
00:48:23,000 --> 00:48:28,520
So I'd like to clip off one or two items here.

1003
00:48:28,520 --> 00:48:33,880
And then the remaining problem will be one of my subproblems.

1004
00:48:33,880 --> 00:48:37,600
I'll be able to recursively call b of something smaller than i.

1005
00:48:37,600 --> 00:48:39,760
Or sorry, b of something larger than i

1006
00:48:39,760 --> 00:48:44,060
will be a smaller subsequence, because we're starting later.

1007
00:48:44,060 --> 00:48:44,880
So what could I do?

1008
00:48:44,880 --> 00:48:47,360
Well, the idea is to just look at pin i

1009
00:48:47,360 --> 00:48:50,280
and think, what could I do to pin i?

1010
00:48:50,280 --> 00:48:52,440
I could not hit it ever with a ball.

1011
00:48:52,440 --> 00:48:53,580
I could skip it.

1012
00:48:53,580 --> 00:48:55,080
That's one option.

1013
00:48:55,080 --> 00:48:57,900
What would be my score then?

1014
00:48:57,900 --> 00:49:02,920
Well, if I skip pin i, that leaves the remaining pins,

1015
00:49:02,920 --> 00:49:04,600
which is just a smaller suffix.

1016
00:49:04,600 --> 00:49:08,980
So that is b of i plus 1.

1017
00:49:08,980 --> 00:49:10,360
I'm going to write a max out here,

1018
00:49:10,360 --> 00:49:12,020
because I'd like to maximize my score.

1019
00:49:12,020 --> 00:49:14,300
And one of the options is, forget about pin i.

1020
00:49:14,300 --> 00:49:16,320
Just solve the rest.

1021
00:49:16,320 --> 00:49:21,660
Another option is I throw a ball, and I exactly hit pin i.

1022
00:49:21,660 --> 00:49:22,320
That's a thing.

1023
00:49:22,320 --> 00:49:22,820
I could do.

1024
00:49:22,820 --> 00:49:26,380
And it would leave exactly the same remainder.

1025
00:49:26,380 --> 00:49:34,700
So another option is b of i plus 1 plus vi.

1026
00:49:34,700 --> 00:49:36,320
Why would I prefer this over this?

1027
00:49:36,320 --> 00:49:39,880
Well, if vi is negative, I'd prefer this.

1028
00:49:39,880 --> 00:49:41,480
But if vi is positive, I'd actually

1029
00:49:41,480 --> 00:49:42,960
prefer this over that.

1030
00:49:42,960 --> 00:49:47,680
So you can figure out which is better just locally.

1031
00:49:47,680 --> 00:49:50,100
But then there's another thing I can do, which is maybe I

1032
00:49:50,100 --> 00:49:50,900
hit this pin.

1033
00:49:52,320 --> 00:49:55,560
And I can do this in a pair with some other pin.

1034
00:49:55,560 --> 00:49:57,320
Now, there's no pin to the left of this one.

1035
00:49:57,320 --> 00:49:59,500
We're assuming we only have the suffix.

1036
00:49:59,500 --> 00:50:02,560
And so the only other thing I can do is throw a ball

1037
00:50:02,560 --> 00:50:04,880
and hit i together with i plus 1.

1038
00:50:04,880 --> 00:50:06,700
And then I get the product.

1039
00:50:06,700 --> 00:50:08,320
Now, what pins remain?

1040
00:50:08,320 --> 00:50:09,480
i plus 2 on.

1041
00:50:09,480 --> 00:50:11,080
Still a suffix.

1042
00:50:11,080 --> 00:50:13,200
So if I remove one or two items, of course,

1043
00:50:13,200 --> 00:50:14,440
I still get a suffix.

1044
00:50:14,440 --> 00:50:17,080
In this case, b of i plus 2.

1045
00:50:17,080 --> 00:50:21,440
And then the number of points that I add on are vi times vi

1046
00:50:21,440 --> 00:50:22,160
plus 1.

1047
00:50:22,160 --> 00:50:26,200
So this is a max of three things.

1048
00:50:26,200 --> 00:50:28,319
So how long does it take me to compute it?

1049
00:50:28,319 --> 00:50:30,440
I claim constant time.

1050
00:50:30,440 --> 00:50:32,060
If I don't count the time it takes

1051
00:50:32,060 --> 00:50:34,480
to compute these other subproblems, which are smaller

1052
00:50:34,480 --> 00:50:38,540
because they are smaller suffixes further to the right,

1053
00:50:38,540 --> 00:50:42,440
then I'm doing a couple of additions, product, max.

1054
00:50:42,440 --> 00:50:43,879
These are all nice numbers.

1055
00:50:43,879 --> 00:50:48,319
They will assume that they live in the w-bit word

1056
00:50:48,319 --> 00:50:50,399
because they were only doing constant size products.

1057
00:50:50,399 --> 00:50:51,500
That's good.

1058
00:50:51,500 --> 00:50:56,700
So this takes constant non-recursive work.

1059
00:50:56,700 --> 00:50:58,200
How many subproblems are?

1060
00:50:58,200 --> 00:51:01,280
Well, it's suffixes, so it's a linear number of subproblems.

1061
00:51:01,280 --> 00:51:05,220
And so the time I'm going to end up meeting

1062
00:51:05,220 --> 00:51:09,220
is the number of subproblems n times

1063
00:51:09,220 --> 00:51:12,540
the non-recursive work I do per subproblem, which is constant.

1064
00:51:12,540 --> 00:51:16,260
And so this is linear time.

1065
00:51:16,260 --> 00:51:17,180
Great.

1066
00:51:17,180 --> 00:51:18,780
Now, I didn't finish sort bot.

1067
00:51:18,780 --> 00:51:21,300
So there's another t, which is to make sure

1068
00:51:21,300 --> 00:51:24,300
that there is a topological order.

1069
00:51:24,300 --> 00:51:27,540
And that is in decreasing i order.

1070
00:51:30,780 --> 00:51:37,560
Or I might write that as a for loop for i equals n, n minus 1.

1071
00:51:37,560 --> 00:51:41,680
This is the order that I would compute my problems

1072
00:51:41,680 --> 00:51:44,940
because the suffix starting at n is the empty suffix.

1073
00:51:44,940 --> 00:51:46,680
The suffix starting at 0, that's the one

1074
00:51:46,680 --> 00:51:47,760
I actually want to compute.

1075
00:51:47,760 --> 00:51:50,500
That's the final suffix I should be computing.

1076
00:51:50,500 --> 00:51:56,520
And then we have a b for base case, which is that first case,

1077
00:51:56,520 --> 00:52:02,100
b of n equals 0 because there's no pins,

1078
00:52:02,100 --> 00:52:04,440
so I don't get any points.

1079
00:52:04,440 --> 00:52:04,940
Sad.

1080
00:52:08,340 --> 00:52:09,220
So this is it.

1081
00:52:09,220 --> 00:52:12,300
We just take these components, plug them

1082
00:52:12,300 --> 00:52:14,720
into this recursive memoized algorithm,

1083
00:52:14,720 --> 00:52:17,020
and we have a linear time algorithm.

1084
00:52:17,020 --> 00:52:19,360
I want to briefly mention a different way you could plug

1085
00:52:19,360 --> 00:52:20,380
together those pieces.

1086
00:52:20,380 --> 00:52:21,960
Which is called bottom-up DP.

1087
00:52:25,220 --> 00:52:29,520
Which is, let's do it for this example.

1088
00:52:29,520 --> 00:52:37,960
So if I have, let's see, let me start with the base case,

1089
00:52:37,960 --> 00:52:39,780
b of n equals 0.

1090
00:52:39,780 --> 00:52:41,560
But now it's an assignment.

1091
00:52:41,560 --> 00:52:44,240
And I'm going to do the for loop from the topological order

1092
00:52:44,240 --> 00:52:48,960
for i equals n, n minus 1 to 0.

1093
00:52:48,960 --> 00:52:50,280
Now I'm going to do the relation.

1094
00:52:50,380 --> 00:53:02,880
b of i equals max of b of i plus 1, and b of i plus 1 plus vi,

1095
00:53:02,880 --> 00:53:08,660
and b of i plus 2 plus vi, vi plus 1.

1096
00:53:08,660 --> 00:53:12,140
Technically, this only works if i is strictly less than n minus

1097
00:53:12,140 --> 00:53:12,940
1.

1098
00:53:12,940 --> 00:53:16,540
So I should have an if i is less than n minus 1

1099
00:53:16,540 --> 00:53:17,860
for that last part.

1100
00:53:17,860 --> 00:53:20,140
Because I can only do, I can only hit two pins if there's at

1101
00:53:20,140 --> 00:53:22,640
least two pins left.

1102
00:53:22,640 --> 00:53:27,740
And then return b of 0.

1103
00:53:27,740 --> 00:53:29,520
So what I just did is a transformation

1104
00:53:29,520 --> 00:53:35,900
from this sort bot template into a non-recursive algorithm,

1105
00:53:35,900 --> 00:53:40,040
a for loop algorithm, where I wrote my base case first.

1106
00:53:40,040 --> 00:53:43,120
Then I did my topological order.

1107
00:53:43,120 --> 00:53:46,620
Then I did my relation.

1108
00:53:46,620 --> 00:53:49,900
Then at the end, I did my base, not base case, the original base

1109
00:53:49,900 --> 00:53:50,400
case.

1110
00:53:50,400 --> 00:53:51,900
And then I did my for loop algorithm,

1111
00:53:51,900 --> 00:53:53,000
which is the original problem.

1112
00:53:57,000 --> 00:53:59,380
And provided you can write your topological order

1113
00:53:59,380 --> 00:54:01,940
as some for loops, this is actually a great way

1114
00:54:01,940 --> 00:54:03,620
to write down a DP as code.

1115
00:54:03,620 --> 00:54:05,380
If I were going to implement this algorithm,

1116
00:54:05,380 --> 00:54:06,460
I would write it this way.

1117
00:54:06,460 --> 00:54:07,700
Because this is super fast.

1118
00:54:07,700 --> 00:54:09,880
No recursive calls, just one for loop.

1119
00:54:09,880 --> 00:54:11,880
In fact, this is almost a trivial algorithm.

1120
00:54:11,880 --> 00:54:16,280
It's amazing that this solves the bowling problem.

1121
00:54:16,280 --> 00:54:19,440
It's in some sense considering every possible strategy

1122
00:54:19,440 --> 00:54:23,020
that we're using is what we like to call local brute force.

1123
00:54:23,020 --> 00:54:25,280
Where when we think about pin i, we

1124
00:54:25,280 --> 00:54:28,260
look at all of the possible things I could do to pin i.

1125
00:54:28,260 --> 00:54:31,420
Here, there's really only three options of what I could do.

1126
00:54:31,420 --> 00:54:34,860
Now normally, if I tried all the options for pin i,

1127
00:54:34,860 --> 00:54:38,060
and then all the options for i plus 1 and i plus 2 and so on,

1128
00:54:38,060 --> 00:54:39,100
that would be exponential.

1129
00:54:39,100 --> 00:54:40,840
It'd be 3 times 3 times 3.

1130
00:54:40,840 --> 00:54:42,080
That's bad.

1131
00:54:42,080 --> 00:54:45,080
But because I can reuse these subproblems,

1132
00:54:45,080 --> 00:54:47,640
it turns out to only be linear time.

1133
00:54:47,640 --> 00:54:49,240
It's almost like magic.

1134
00:54:49,240 --> 00:54:56,560
dp is essentially an idea of using local brute force.

1135
00:55:02,279 --> 00:55:06,419
And by defining a small number of subproblems up front,

1136
00:55:06,419 --> 00:55:09,439
and as long as I stay within those subproblems,

1137
00:55:09,439 --> 00:55:12,899
as long as I'm always recursing into this polynomial space,

1138
00:55:12,899 --> 00:55:15,580
I end up only doing polynomial work,

1139
00:55:15,580 --> 00:55:17,819
even though I'm in some sense exploring exponentially

1140
00:55:17,819 --> 00:55:18,439
many options.

1141
00:55:19,240 --> 00:55:22,399
And it's because what I do to this pin

1142
00:55:22,399 --> 00:55:26,220
doesn't depend too much to what I do to a pin much later.

1143
00:55:26,220 --> 00:55:31,819
And there's a lot of intuition going on here for when dp works.

1144
00:55:31,819 --> 00:55:36,439
But we're going to see a lot more examples of that coming up.

1145
00:55:36,439 --> 00:55:38,819
And I just want to mention that the intuition for how

1146
00:55:38,819 --> 00:55:41,720
to write a recurrence like this is to think about,

1147
00:55:41,720 --> 00:55:43,200
in the case of suffixes, you always

1148
00:55:43,200 --> 00:55:45,000
want to think about the first item, or maybe

1149
00:55:45,000 --> 00:55:46,279
the first couple items.

1150
00:55:46,279 --> 00:55:48,279
In the case of prefixes, you always think about the last item.

1151
00:55:48,279 --> 00:55:49,220
And in the case of prefixes, you always think about the last item.

1152
00:55:49,240 --> 00:55:52,939
And for substrings, it could be any item, maybe in the middle.

1153
00:55:52,939 --> 00:55:55,020
If I remove an item from the middle of a substring,

1154
00:55:55,020 --> 00:55:57,839
I get two substrings, so I can recurse.

1155
00:55:57,839 --> 00:56:00,100
Here, or in general, what we want to do

1156
00:56:00,100 --> 00:56:03,560
is identify some feature of the solution

1157
00:56:03,560 --> 00:56:06,879
that if we knew that feature, we would be done.

1158
00:56:06,879 --> 00:56:09,479
We would reduce to a smaller subproblem.

1159
00:56:09,479 --> 00:56:11,379
In this case, we just say, well, what

1160
00:56:11,379 --> 00:56:15,180
are the possible things I could do to the first pin?

1161
00:56:15,180 --> 00:56:16,859
There are three options.

1162
00:56:16,859 --> 00:56:19,040
If I knew which option it was, I would be done.

1163
00:56:19,040 --> 00:56:21,660
I could recurse and do my addition.

1164
00:56:21,660 --> 00:56:23,759
Now, I don't know which thing I want to do.

1165
00:56:23,759 --> 00:56:26,640
So just try them all and take the max.

1166
00:56:26,640 --> 00:56:28,340
And if you're maximizing, you take the max.

1167
00:56:28,340 --> 00:56:29,900
If you're minimizing, you take the min.

1168
00:56:29,900 --> 00:56:32,520
Sometimes you take an or or an and.

1169
00:56:32,520 --> 00:56:34,480
There might be some combination function.

1170
00:56:34,480 --> 00:56:36,120
For optimization problems where you're

1171
00:56:36,120 --> 00:56:37,660
trying to maximize or minimize something,

1172
00:56:37,660 --> 00:56:39,500
like shortest paths we're trying to minimize,

1173
00:56:39,500 --> 00:56:40,660
we put a min here.

1174
00:56:40,660 --> 00:56:42,940
So usually it's min or max.

1175
00:56:42,940 --> 00:56:46,259
And this is extremely powerful.

1176
00:56:46,259 --> 00:56:48,400
All you need to do, the hard part,

1177
00:56:49,040 --> 00:56:50,640
is to do the next thing.

1178
00:56:50,640 --> 00:56:53,740
Now, this is the most complex, most complex,

1179
00:56:53,740 --> 00:56:56,600
and most creative, and most exciting part of this.

1180
00:56:56,600 --> 00:57:01,100
And it's the most dynamic part of this inspired design part,

1181
00:57:01,100 --> 00:57:04,860
where you say, what do I need to know that would

1182
00:57:04,860 --> 00:57:06,440
let me solve my problem?

1183
00:57:06,440 --> 00:57:09,440
And if you can identify that and the number of choices

1184
00:57:09,440 --> 00:57:12,540
for what you need to know is polynomial,

1185
00:57:12,540 --> 00:57:16,540
then you will be able to get a polynomial dynamic program.

1186
00:57:16,540 --> 00:57:17,500
That's the intuition.

1187
00:57:17,500 --> 00:57:18,900
We'll see a lot more examples in the next three lectures.

